      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b7f5cd5a_097fef03",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/SignalStrengthController.java",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 75,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-14T02:25:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In SST we were quite limited because \"style of the file\" and of course the SST is handling both control messages (like this) and also things in the critical path of the pollState() procedure where performance is absolutely paramount. In this file, we are only handling a control path, so performance isn\u0027t quite as critical and I think we can prioritize readability.\n\nThus, I\u0027d like to suggest that we move away from using posted messages and pairs. Instead, there are many other patterns that we can use, but one simple option is to simply post runnables. That way you don\u0027t have to spend the code creating a pair and posting the message then un-tangling in the handleMessage(). You can simply use Handler::post(Runnable).\n\nIf you want to be slightly fancier, then rather than using capturing lambdas, you can move one step closer to a statemachine and declare a class for each event that implements runnable, use the constructor to accept the arguments for that event, and then just post the instance of that class. :-) So many options for cleaner code now that we\u0027re not in SST!",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 75,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 75,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "855b161a4225d85bea094ad16964d043d541a5fd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
